{
  "page": 10,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "734 · Basescu et al.",
      "bbox": [
        88.1969985961914,
        51.38132095336914,
        162.49716186523438,
        60.33945846557617
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "3.2.1. RRT generation and spline-based smoothing",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        78.71449279785156,
        343.4388732910156,
        87.43177032470703
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Randomized motion planning has proved to be an eﬀective strategy for generating motion plans in",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        90.59176635742188,
        518.7604370117188,
        99.38874053955078
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "complex environments and in the presence of local minima. Rapidly-exploring Random Trees (RRTs)",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        102.54678344726562,
        518.760498046875,
        111.34375762939453
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "have been a particularly powerful tool for solving path planning problems. However, RRTs often",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        114.50180053710938,
        518.7705078125,
        123.29877471923828
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "exhibit poor performance when required to reason about dynamical systems with large state-spaces.",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        126.45681762695312,
        518.7904052734375,
        135.2537841796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Here, we use a simpliﬁed three-state model to plan a collision-free “ﬂight path” with a rapidly",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        138.41183471679688,
        518.7605590820312,
        147.20880126953125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "exploring random tree in three dimensions.",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        150.36685180664062,
        275.8724060058594,
        159.163818359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Starting with an arbitrary 3D mesh of an environment, binvox (Min, 2019; Nooruddin and",
      "bbox": [
        100.15199279785156,
        162.32284545898438,
        518.7305908203125,
        171.11981201171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Turk, 2003) is used to perform voxelization. The voxelized mesh is then converted to a binary",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        174.27786254882812,
        518.7705078125,
        183.0748291015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "occupancy tree, and OctoMap (Hornung et al., 2013) is used to provide information about proximity",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        186.23287963867188,
        518.760498046875,
        195.02984619140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to obstacles. While the trajectory generated by this process will not be a feasible path for our aircraft",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        198.18789672851562,
        518.8203125,
        206.98486328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to follow, it attempts to provide a receding-horizon goal point for the next stage of the planner and",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        210.14291381835938,
        518.7704467773438,
        218.93988037109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "helps the trajectory optimizer avoid local minima. Initially, we study the motion planning problem",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        222.09793090820312,
        518.7605590820312,
        230.8948974609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "apart from the perception problem and assume that we have a full map of the environment. In",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        234.05392456054688,
        518.7605590820312,
        242.85089111328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Section 7, we extend our approach to the case where the ﬁeld-of-view is limited and trajectories",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        246.00894165039062,
        518.79052734375,
        254.805908203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "must be replanned when provided with new information about the environment.",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        257.9639587402344,
        439.1494445800781,
        266.76092529296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The ﬂight path is generated through a combination of standard RRT (Lavalle, 1998), spline-based",
      "bbox": [
        100.15199279785156,
        269.9189758300781,
        518.75048828125,
        278.7159423828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "smoothing satisfying a maximum curvature constraint (Yang and Sukkarieh, 2010), and a ﬁnal",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        281.87396240234375,
        518.7306518554688,
        290.6709289550781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "reparameterization of the trajectory. This combination of steps provides a path which is more",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        293.8299560546875,
        518.7706909179688,
        302.6269226074219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "dynamically feasible than a raw RRT and also helps to intelligently select an goal point for the",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        305.7849426269531,
        518.7605590820312,
        314.5819091796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "trajectory optimizer.",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        317.73992919921875,
        178.2688446044922,
        326.5368957519531
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "First, the RRT is grown in a three dimensional state space with a 10% bias towards the goal",
      "bbox": [
        100.15199279785156,
        329.6949157714844,
        518.7306518554688,
        338.49188232421875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "point, until it arrives within an error ball around the goal. The resultant path x = [x0, x1, . . . , xn]",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        341.60009765625,
        518.5855712890625,
        351.3620300292969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "from start to goal is then pruned as described in (Yang and Sukkarieh, 2008) to remove extraneous",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        353.6048889160156,
        518.7803955078125,
        362.40185546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "nodes.",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        365.5608825683594,
        115.67384338378906,
        374.35784912109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We then employ G2 Continuous Cubic Bézier Spiral Path Smoothing (G2CBS) to obtain a",
      "bbox": [
        100.15199279785156,
        377.515869140625,
        518.7207641601562,
        386.3128356933594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "continuous curvature, smooth path (Yang and Sukkarieh, 2010). This algorithm examines each set",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        389.4708557128906,
        518.7604370117188,
        398.267822265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of three adjacent nodes [W1, W2, W3] in the pruned path. It transforms them into a local 2D space,",
      "bbox": [
        88.19699096679688,
        401.37603759765625,
        518.6982421875,
        411.136962890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "computes control points for two symmetric G2 splines satisfying a maximum curvature constraint",
      "bbox": [
        88.19697570800781,
        413.3808288574219,
        518.7606201171875,
        422.17779541015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "κmax (set to 2 m−1), then transforms the control points back into 3D. The output of this algorithm",
      "bbox": [
        88.19697570800781,
        423.3547058105469,
        518.73046875,
        435.6277770996094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "is a set of 3D control points for each spline,",
      "bbox": [
        88.19697570800781,
        437.29180908203125,
        278.2733459472656,
        446.0887756347656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Bc =",
      "bbox": [
        88.19697570800781,
        456.40380859375,
        111.38187408447266,
        466.69476318359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
